# tcpdrill -- the retransmission timeout, now that it is measured.
#
# retransmit.drill asserts that the interval DOUBLES.  This file asserts what
# it doubles FROM: NX_ENABLE_TCP_RTT_ESTIMATOR makes nx_tcp_socket_timeout_rate
# the RFC 6298 section 2 estimate rather than the fixed one second every socket
# used to get, so the same ladder now starts from whatever the path measured.
#
# HOW A ROUND TRIP IS INDUCED.  `idle MS` between a segment leaving and the
# acknowledgment being injected is the round trip the stack sees -- it is the
# only delay in the loop, because the peer is inside the guest.  The delay has
# to stay under the timeout currently armed or the segment is retransmitted
# first, which is a different case (rto02), so the delay here is 600 ms
# against the one second the socket starts with.
#
# WHAT THE NUMBERS COME OUT AT.  A first sample of R gives SRTT = R and
# RTTVAR = R/2, so RTO = R + 4(R/2) = 3R.  600 ms of idle plus the milliseconds
# either side of it is about 31 ticks of the 50 Hz IP timer, so
# the first timeout is about 1.9 seconds where the old fixed base was 1.0 --
# a separation wide enough that the bounds below never have to be tight.
#
# ALL TIMINGS ARE RELATIVE TO THE PREVIOUS MATCHED FRAME, from the E-Clock
# stamp the tap device takes inside BeginIO.  Bounds are wide for the same
# reason retransmit.drill's are: what is asserted is which of two intervals
# the stack chose, not a stopwatch reading.
#
# EVERY CASE ENDS WITH A RESET from the peer, so a socket left retransmitting
# cannot leak into the next case.  retransmit.drill explains why at length.
#
# SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT

# RFC 6298 2.2.  One acknowledged segment is one measurement, and the timeout
# armed for the next segment is three times it.  Without the estimator this
# second interval is 1.0 s and this case fails on "nothing was sent at all".
case rto01_first_sample_sets_the_timeout
socket
connect
tx S seq=0
rx SA seq=0 ack=1 win=8192 mss=1460
tx A seq=1 ack=1
send 100
tx PA seq=1 ack=1 len=100 within=300
idle 600
rx A seq=1 ack=101 win=8192
send 100
tx PA seq=101 ack=1 len=100 within=300
tx PA seq=101 ack=1 len=100 after=1400 within=2600
rx R seq=101

# RFC 6298 3, Karn's algorithm.  The segment is retransmitted before it is
# acknowledged, so the acknowledgment could be answering either copy and is
# not a measurement.  If it were taken, it would read as roughly 1.2 s and the
# next timeout would be three times that; it must still be the one second base.
case rto02_a_retransmitted_segment_is_not_measured
socket
connect
tx S seq=0
rx SA seq=0 ack=1 win=8192 mss=1460
tx A seq=1 ack=1
send 100
tx PA seq=1 ack=1 len=100 within=300
tx PA seq=1 ack=1 len=100 after=700 within=1400
rx A seq=1 ack=101 win=8192
send 100
tx PA seq=101 ack=1 len=100 within=300
tx PA seq=101 ack=1 len=100 after=700 within=1400
rx R seq=101

# RFC 6298 2.4.  The peer answers at once, so the measurement is a tick or two
# and three times it is under a tenth of a second.  The floor keeps the timeout
# at one second -- which is what this lab's own links produce, and why the
# estimator is not expected to change anything measurable here.
case rto03_the_one_second_floor_holds
socket
connect
tx S seq=0
rx SA seq=0 ack=1 win=8192 mss=1460
tx A seq=1 ack=1
send 100
tx PA seq=1 ack=1 len=100 within=300
rx A seq=1 ack=101 win=8192
send 100
tx PA seq=101 ack=1 len=100 within=300
tx PA seq=101 ack=1 len=100 after=700 within=1400
rx R seq=101

# RFC 6298 5.5 on top of section 2: the ladder is unchanged, it merely starts
# somewhere else.  Two intervals, about 1.9 s and 3.8 s rather than 1 s
# and 2 s.
case rto04_backoff_doubles_the_measured_base
socket
connect
tx S seq=0
rx SA seq=0 ack=1 win=8192 mss=1460
tx A seq=1 ack=1
send 100
tx PA seq=1 ack=1 len=100 within=300
idle 600
rx A seq=1 ack=101 win=8192
send 100
tx PA seq=101 ack=1 len=100 within=300
tx PA seq=101 ack=1 len=100 after=1400 within=2600
tx PA seq=101 ack=1 len=100 after=2800 within=5200
rx R seq=101

# RFC 6298 2.3.  The first sample is deliberately pessimistic -- RTTVAR is set
# to half of R with nothing to average against -- and later samples of the same
# path narrow it by a quarter each time.  Six exchanges over the same 600 ms
# path therefore end up near the floor rather than at rto01's 1.9 s, which is
# the difference between the section 2.2 initialisation and the section 2.3
# update showing up on the wire.
#
# This case does NOT discriminate on its own: the estimate narrows to the floor,
# and the floor is the second a build without the estimator uses, so the bound
# below passes either way.  What it asserts is the CONTRAST with rto01 -- same
# script, same path, 1.9 s there and 1.0 s here -- and it is the one case in
# this file that would catch an estimate that never narrowed.
case rto05_the_estimate_narrows_on_a_steady_path
socket
connect
tx S seq=0
rx SA seq=0 ack=1 win=8192 mss=1460
tx A seq=1 ack=1
send 100
tx PA seq=1 ack=1 len=100 within=300
idle 600
rx A seq=1 ack=101 win=8192
send 100
tx PA seq=101 ack=1 len=100 within=300
idle 600
rx A seq=1 ack=201 win=8192
send 100
tx PA seq=201 ack=1 len=100 within=300
idle 600
rx A seq=1 ack=301 win=8192
send 100
tx PA seq=301 ack=1 len=100 within=300
idle 600
rx A seq=1 ack=401 win=8192
send 100
tx PA seq=401 ack=1 len=100 within=300
idle 600
rx A seq=1 ack=501 win=8192
send 100
tx PA seq=501 ack=1 len=100 within=300
idle 600
rx A seq=1 ack=601 win=8192
send 100
tx PA seq=601 ack=1 len=100 within=300
tx PA seq=601 ack=1 len=100 after=800 within=1600
rx R seq=601
